    {
      "title": "Copy-on-Write and Choosing Polars or DuckDB",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 4,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Copy-on-Write and Choosing Polars or DuckDB",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Copy-on-write in pandas 3",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "==Copy-on-write== is the **only** behaviour in pandas 3",
                "Every operation result acts as a ==separate copy==",
                "Data is physically copied only when you **modify** it (lazy copy)",
                "`y = x['a']; y.iloc[0] = 9` leaves `x` unchanged, so views are safe"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "python",
              "lines": [
                "df['a'][mask] = 1      # no longer changes df",
                "df[mask]['a'] = 1      # never changed df",
                "df.loc[mask, 'a'] = 1  # one step: works"
              ],
              "note": "chained assignment vs .loc"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Chained assignment does nothing to `df`; pandas 3 warns with `ChainedAssignmentError`",
                "`SettingWithCopyWarning` is {green:gone}",
                "No more defensive `.copy()` calls"
              ],
              "style": "cross",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Polars or DuckDB?",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Tool",
                "Switch when",
                "Key calls"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "**pandas**",
                  "small data, plotting, scikit-learn, matplotlib",
                  "stay put"
                ],
                [
                  "**Polars**",
                  "multi-core speed, ==lazy== optimiser, ~1-100 GB, lower memory",
                  "`scan_parquet`, `.lazy()`, `.collect()`"
                ],
                [
                  "**DuckDB**",
                  "SQL-shaped work, out-of-core, files in place",
                  "`duckdb.sql(...)`, `.df()`"
                ]
              ],
              "highlightRow": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "python",
              "lines": [
                "import duckdb",
                "duckdb.sql(\"select city, avg(x) from df group by city\").df()"
              ],
              "note": "queries the pandas frame df in place, returns a DataFrame"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Remember",
              "text": "Write with ==df.loc[mask, col] = v== in one step. Polars = fast and lazy; DuckDB = SQL over frames and Parquet."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
